# Frontend Development Standards

## Stack

- React 18 + TypeScript + Vite
- React Query for server state
- Zustand for client state (when needed)
- React Router v6
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- React Hook Form + Zod for forms
- i18next for internationalization scaffolding

## Component Rules

- Functional components always
- One component per file
- Props typed with TypeScript (never `any`)
- Separate logic into custom hooks
- Pure components when possible
- Co-locate tests with components

## Design System

- If the consuming repository has a root `DESIGN.md`, treat it as the
  project-local visual identity contract.
- If `DESIGN.md` is absent, inspect local Storybook, token files, Tailwind
  config, shadcn theme, UI docs, ADRs, screenshots, and existing components
  before choosing visual styles.
- If those local design sources exist, treat them as input for root
  `DESIGN.md` standardization. A frontend plan may use them temporarily, but it
  must record the missing `DESIGN.md` as a design-standardization gap unless
  creating it is already in scope.
- Do not invent a new palette, typography scale, spacing scale, radius scale,
  or component variant when project-local tokens or components already exist.
- For UI tickets, implementation plans must record a `Design System Contract`
  in `Execution Notes for Implementer`, including source, impacted
  tokens/components, planned impact, and visual validation evidence.
- Any intentional design-system change must be explicitly scoped and mapped to
  an acceptance criterion, related work item, or documented risk.
- Follow `design-system-standards.mdc` for DESIGN.md usage, fallback behavior,
  and validation expectations.

## API Contract

- If the consuming repository has `docs/api-contract.md`, `contracts/api/`,
  or `contracts/api/api-contract-source.json`, treat those files as the
  project-local API source of truth for frontend planning and implementation.
- Use `consume-api-contract` before adding or changing API clients, server
  state hooks, mocked backend flows, forms, permissions, or capability-gated
  UI.
- Do not invent endpoints, payload fields, status codes, auth rules, feature
  flags, or response shapes. Missing behavior is a contract gap or blocker.
- Generated API contract files in a UI repository are read-only; update the API
  source repository and rerun `api-contract-sync` when the contract must
  change.

## Performance

- Initial bundle < 250KB
- Lazy loading for routes
- Core Web Vitals in green
- Optimized images

## Testing

- Vitest + React Testing Library
- Test behavior, not implementation details
- Minimum coverage: 80%
- No testing internal implementation details

## Accessibility

- WCAG AA minimum
- Semantic HTML always
- `aria-labels` where needed
- Keyboard navigation functional
- Color contrast compliant
- Focus, hover, active, disabled, loading, empty, error, and permission states
  are covered when relevant to the acceptance criteria

## Package Manager

- PNPM always (not npm or yarn)
